[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609108000-a-character-literal-inverted-the-scanner"
 :adr/doc_type "adr"
 :adr/topic "a-character-literal-inverted-the-scanner"
 :adr/title "A character literal inverted a source scanner's state, in two of the nineteen that this workspace hand-writes"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-10"
 :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"]
 :adr/repos ["com-junkawasaki/root"]
 :adr/related ["adr-2609107000-everything-is-a-form-arranged-against-what-runs"]
 :adr/validation {:measured "paired-probe survey of 19 hand-written scanners; corpus before/after on orgs"
                  :route "nbb, no JVM"
                  :result "2 vulnerable, 9 safe, 8 unmeasured; corpus identical before and after"}
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nWriting a new detector on 2026-09-10 (`verify-printed-value-identity`), the\ndetector reported four hits in its own docstrings. The cause was not the rule\nbut the scanner underneath it: a Clojure CHARACTER LITERAL -- the `\\\"` in\n`(= c \\\")` -- was read as the start of a string literal, and from that point\nthe scanner's string/code state was inverted for the rest of the file.\n\nThe scanner had been copied from `verify-codec-seq-expansion`, which is\nregistered in `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` and runs daily. So the question was\nnot whether one new file had a bug, but how far the shape had spread.\n\n## What was measured\n\n**Nineteen scripts under `scripts/` carry a hand-written source scanner** (a\n`loop` with an `in-str?` state). Grepping for a `(+ i 2)` skip was tried first\nand abandoned: the presence of that expression says nothing about whether the\ncharacter-literal case is handled. They were measured behaviourally instead.\n\nThe probe is a pair of inputs identical except for one character literal:\n\n    A  (defn f [c] (if (= c \\\") 1 2))     B  (defn f [c] (if (= c 34) 1 2))\n       (defn g [] \"TAIL\")                    (defn g [] \"TAIL\")\n\nA scanner that handles character literals treats the tail the same way in both.\nOne that does not, inverts.\n\n| result | count | |\n|---|---|---|\n| **differs between A and B** | **2** | `verify-codec-seq-expansion/top-level-forms`, `migrate-clojure-string-to-kotoba-text/code-only` |\n| same in both | 9 | measured safe |\n| not measured | 8 | the function takes two arguments (an index) or the harness could not call it; **not clean, unmeasured** |\n\n`top-level-forms` counted the two-form input as **one form**: everything after\nthe character literal was swallowed. `code-only` is worse in kind -- it is the\nfunction a source-REWRITING migration script uses to decide what is code and\nwhat is prose, and its own docstring exists because \"a text search was asked a\nquestion about code\". With the character literal present, `\"TAIL\"` survived as\ncode rather than being blanked. The boundary that function defends had\ninverted.\n\n## Decision\n\nBoth are fixed the same way: in the outer state, a backslash consumes the next\ncharacter. That is also what keeps `\\)` from being counted as a paren.\n\n`verify-codec-seq-expansion` additionally gained the string-blanking pass the\nnew detector has, because it was detecting its own fixtures, and an extra\nself-check fixture: a two-form input containing `(= c \\\")` whose second form\ncarries a known-bad shape. **Removing the fix now makes the detector REFUSE\n(exit 2) rather than quietly report less** -- verified in both directions.\n\n## What changed in the corpus: nothing\n\nMeasured on `orgs/` before and after: `SCANNED 119`, `FINDINGS 4`, the same\nfour files at the same four line numbers. **This is a latent fix, not a\nbehaviour change**, and it is recorded as one rather than as \"N wrong findings\ncorrected\".\n\nThe distortion was real but local to files the registered target does not\nscan. On the detector's own file the unpatched scanner reported one finding at\nline 73; patched, it reports the fixtures at 172 and 188. A wrong line number\nis what the bug produces before it produces a wrong count.\n\n## What is NOT claimed\n\n- The eight two-argument scanners were NOT measured. They are unmeasured, not\n  safe, and no one has run the pair probe against them.\n- No finding anywhere was shown to be wrong because of this bug. The corpus is\n  unchanged; the evidence is the paired probe and the detector's own file.\n- `migrate-clojure-string-to-kotoba-text` has no self-test and did not gain\n  one. Its fix is verified by the same paired probe, run against the extracted\n  function.\n- Nothing checks that a new hand-written scanner handles character literals.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nExtract each scanner and run the pair; a safe scanner answers the same for both:\n\n    A = \"(defn f [c] (if (= c \\\\\\\") 1 2))\\n(defn g [] \\\"TAIL\\\")\"\n    B = \"(defn f [c] (if (= c 34) 1 2))\\n(defn g [] \\\"TAIL\\\")\"\n\nFor the detector, the control is the fix itself: delete the `(= c \\\\)` branch\nfrom `top-level-forms` and it exits 2 with \"self-check failed\", not 0.\n"}]
